  "AI content payment is not merely a copyright or transaction-efficiency problem; it is a question of political-economic legitimacy. As AI valuations reach hundreds of billions, society asks why the gains from public web content and human knowledge accrue to a few firms. The paper proposes a six-layer tiered data market — from free public content through scarce verified datasets to public benefit-sharing — and defines the AI network democratic economy: rights, pricing, participation, auditability, and returns, each democratized through machine-readable protocols like AICR/AICL.": "Kaboomwanean Kanoa ibukin te AI Bon Tiaki tii te Kangaanga n Copyright ke n Transaction-Efficiency; Bon te Titiraki ibukin Legitimacy n Political-Economy. Ngkai a roko Kabooani AI n tabun Hundred Billion, e titirakina Botannaomata bwa e aera ngkai a rin Booboo mai Kanoa aika Public i aon te Web ao Ataibwaia Aomata i naanoia tii Company tabeua. E anga te Beeba te Makete n Data ae Onoua Kanoana ao Tiered — man Kanoa aika Public ao Akea Boona nakon Datasets aika a Karako ma a Kakoauaki, nakon Tibwatibwaan Booboo nakoia Aomata ni Kabaneia — ao e katereterea Economics n te Network n Democratic ibukin te AI: Riai, Baairean Booboo, Rinan Aomata Iai, Auditability, ao Manga-Karekeaki, ake a Democratized nako tatabemaniia rinanon Protocols aika a Kona ni Warekaki irouni Mesin n aroni AICR/AICL.",
